al can be given by the ratio of the products of the distances from the incenter to the adjacent vertices. This leads to the formula:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{AB}{CD} = \\frac{IA \\cdot IB}{IC \\cdot ID}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Calculation**:\n   - Substituting the given distances into the formula:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{AB}{CD} = \\frac{IA \\cdot IB}{IC \\cdot ID} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 7}{4 \\cdot 9} = \\frac{35}{36}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\frac{AB}{CD}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{35}{36}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understand the problem and given data:** \n\n   We are given a quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) inscribed with a circle \\(\\omega\\) that has center \\(I\\). The distances from \\(I\\) to the vertices of the quadrilateral \\(IA = 5\\), \\(IB = 7\\), \\(IC = 4\\), and \\(ID = 9\\). We need to find the value of \\(\\frac{AB}{CD}\\).\n\n2. **Identify key properties of the inscribed circle:**\n\n   Since \\(ABCD\\) has an inscribed circle, it implies that \\(ABCD\\) is a tangential quadrilateral. In such a quadrilateral, the sum of the lengths of opposite sides are equal. However, this property is not immediately necessary for this problem.\n   \n3. **Consider triangles \\(AIB\\) and \\(CID\\):**\n   \n   The areas of these triangles can be expressed in terms of the lengths of the sides connected to the center \\(I\\). We use the following trigonometric formulation:\n   \\[\n   [AIB] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot IA \\cdot IB \\cdot \\sin(\\angle AIB)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   [CID] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot IC \\cdot ID \\cdot \\sin(\\angle CID)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Note the equivalence of the angles \\(\\sin(\\angle AIB)\\) and \\(\\sin(\\angle CID):**\n\n   Since \\(IA, IB, IC,\\) and \\(ID\\) are angle bisectors, we also note: \n   \\[\n   \\angle AIB + \\angle CID = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(\\angle AIB) = \\sin(\\angle CID)\n   \\]\n5. **Equate the height related by the radius of the inscribed circle:**\n\n   The inscribed circle implies that the radius is the same for circles passing through every side due to symmetry. Hence, the height from \\(I\\) perpendicular to \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) (altitudes) is the same.\n\n6. **Relate the areas of triangles involving \\(I\\):**\n\n   Since we have the same height, the ratio of areas of \\(\\triangle AIB\\) to \\(\\triangle CID\\) is equal to the ratio of the bases \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\):\n   \\[\n    \\frac{[AIB]}{[CID]}=\\frac{AB}{CD}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Express the areas in terms of \\(IA, IB, IC, ID\\):**\n   \n   Using:\n   \\[\n    [AIB] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot IA \\cdot IB \\cdot \\sin(\\angle AIB) \n   \\]\n   \\[\n    [CID] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot IC \\cdot ID \\cdot \\sin(\\angle CID)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{[AIB]}{[CID]} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot IA \\cdot IB \\cdot \\sin (\\angle AIB)}{\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot IC \\cdot ID \\cdot \\sin (\\angle CID)}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sin(\\angle AIB) = \\sin(\\angle CID)\\), then:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{[AIB]}{[CID]} = \\frac{IA \\cdot IB}{IC \\cdot ID}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Substitute the given values:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{IA \\cdot IB}{IC \\cdot ID} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 7}{4 \\cdot 9}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5 \\cdot 7}{4 \\cdot 9} = \\frac{35}{36}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Conclusion:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{35}{36}}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The plane is covered by a grid of squares with a side length of 1. Is it possible to construct an equilateral triangle with vertices at the grid points?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the question is whether it's possible to construct an equilateral triangle with all three vertices at the grid points on a plane covered by squares of side length 1. Hmm, interesting. Let me try to work through this step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that an equilateral triangle has all sides equal and all angles equal to 60 degrees. The vertices need to be at points where the grid lines intersect, which are points with integer