9, sin(1.5)\u22480.997494. So f(x)\u22480.997499 > sin x\u22480.997494.\n\nSo f(x) is slightly larger than sin x here. Therefore, it's still true that f(x) > sin x for all x \u2208 [0, \u03c0], with equality approached as x approaches some point near 1.5 radians but not exactly achieved.\n\nTherefore, since g(x) = sin(sin x) \u2264 sin x (because sin y \u2264 y for y \u22650, and sin x \u2208 [0,1]), and f(x) > sin x for all x \u2208 [0, \u03c0], then f(x) > g(x) in [0, \u03c0]. And in [\u03c0, 2\u03c0], as before, g(x) is negative and f(x) is positive. Therefore, overall, f(x) > g(x) for all real x.\n\nHence, the conclusion is that for all real numbers x, cos(cos x) is greater than sin(sin x).\n\n**Final Answer**\nFor all real numbers \\( x \\), \\( \\cos(\\cos x) \\) is greater than \\( \\sin(\\sin x) \\). Thus, the comparison is \\(\\boxed{f(x) > g(x)}\\) for all \\( x \\in \\mathbf{R} \\).", "deepseek_solution": "To compare the functions \\( f(x) = \\cos(\\cos x) \\) and \\( g(x) = \\sin(\\sin x) \\) for all real numbers \\( x \\):\n\n1. **Range Analysis**:\n   - The range of \\( \\cos x \\) is \\([-1, 1]\\), so \\( \\cos(\\cos x) \\) ranges from \\( \\cos(1) \\) (approximately 0.5403) to 1. Thus, \\( f(x) \\in [\\cos(1), 1] \\).\n   - The range of \\( \\sin x \\) is \\([-1, 1]\\), so \\( \\sin(\\sin x) \\) ranges from \\(-\\sin(1)\\) (approximately -0.8415) to \\(\\sin(1)\\) (approximately 0.8415). Thus, \\( g(x) \\in [-\\sin(1), \\sin(1)] \\).\n\n2. **Periodicity and Symmetry**:\n   - Both functions are periodic with period \\( 2\\pi \\).\n   - \\( f(x) \\) is even, and \\( g(x) \\) is odd.\n\n3. **Behavior Over Key Intervals**:\n   - In the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\), \\( g(x) \\) is non-negative, while \\( f(x) \\) is always positive.\n   - In the interval \\([\\pi, 2\\pi]\\), \\( g(x) \\) is non-positive, while \\( f(x) \\) remains positive.\n\n4. **Critical Points and Comparison**:\n   - At \\( x = 0 \\), \\( x = \\pi \\), and \\( x = 2\\pi \\), \\( f(x) = \