it, no. If the graph intersects the x-axis, that means it has real roots. So, first, the quadratic must have at least one real root. So, discriminant D \u2265 0. Second, at least one of those roots must be negative.\n\nAlternatively, if the quadratic has two real roots, at least one of them is negative. But maybe even if there's only one real root (a repeated root), it has to be negative. So, combining these cases, we need to ensure that either:\n\n1. There are two real roots, and at least one is negative.\n\nOR\n\n2. There is one real root (repeated), and it's negative.\n\nSo, the approach would be:\n\n- First, find when the quadratic has real roots (D \u2265 0).\n\n- Then, check the conditions under which at least one root is negative.\n\nBut perhaps we can use the properties of quadratic equations. Let me recall that if a quadratic has roots of different signs (one positive, one negative), then the product of the roots (c/a) is negative. If both roots are negative, then the sum of the roots (-b/a) is positive and the product (c/a) is positive. But since we need at least one negative root, we can consider two cases:\n\nCase 1: Exactly one root is negative. This happens when the product of the roots is negative (so c/a < 0).\n\nCase 2: Both roots are negative. This happens when the product is positive (c/a > 0) and the sum is positive (-b/a > 0).\n\nBut since the problem says \"at least once,\" either case 1 or case 2 will satisfy the condition. So, combining these two cases, the conditions would be:\n\nEither\n\n1. c/a < 0 (one positive, one negative root)\n\nOR\n\n2. c/a > 0 and -b/a > 0 (both roots negative)\n\nAdditionally, in both cases, the discriminant must be non-negative (D \u2265 0).\n\nBut since in case 1, when c/a < 0, even if the discriminant is positive (so two real roots), we have one positive and one negative root, so that's acceptable. In case 2, discriminant must be \u2265 0 as well, and both roots negative. So, perhaps we can model these conditio