m \u2264 -6 or m \u22651, but m \u22602.\n\nBut the answer options don't include m \u2264 -6. The options are A (1<m<3), B (1\u2264m<2 or 2<m<3), C (m<1), D (m>3). So maybe the correct answer is among these. Let's proceed.\n\nNow, moving on to the second part: ensuring at least one root is negative. For that, as mentioned earlier, we can use the cases based on the coefficients.\n\nBut another approach is to note that a quadratic intersects the negative x-axis if either:\n\n1. It has two real roots, and at least one is negative.\n\nOR\n\n2. It has one real root (repeated root) which is negative.\n\nBut in the case of a repeated root, the root is at x = -b/(2a). So, we need that x-coordinate to be negative.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps combining all possibilities:\n\nTo ensure that the quadratic crosses the negative x-axis at least once, it's necessary that either:\n\n- There are two real roots, with at least one negative.\n\nOR\n\n- There is one real root (with multiplicity two) which is negative.\n\nBut how to model this.\n\nAlternatively, think of the quadratic's value at x=0. If f(0) and the leading coefficient have opposite signs, then the parabola crosses the x-axis on both sides of the y-axis (since if a >0 and f(0) <0, it opens upwards and is below the x-axis at x=0, so must cross both sides; similarly if a <0 and f(0) >0, it opens downward and is above the x-axis at x=0, so crosses both sides). However, if f(0) and the leading coefficient have the same sign, then the roots are either both positive or both negative, depending on the axis direction.\n\nBut maybe this approach is useful. Let's compute f(0). For f(x) = (m-2)x\u00b2 -4mx +2m -6, f(0) = 2m -6. So, f(0) = 2(m - 3). So, the sign of f(0) depends on m. If m >3, f(0) is positive; if m <3, f(0) is negative; if m=3, f(0)=0.\n\nIf the leading coefficient a = m -2. So, a >0 when m >2; a <0 when m <2.\n\nSo, the product a*f(0) = (m -2)*2(m -3). The sign of this product:\n\nIf a*f(0) <0, then the quadratic cross