. \n\n= 24/z - 12 z + (24 * 13 / 60) z - (12 * 13 / 60) z^3 + ... \n\nSimplify coefficients:\n\n24/z + [ -12 z + (312 / 60) z ] + ... = 24/z + [ -12 + 5.2 ] z + ... = 24/z - 6.8 z + ... \n\nTherefore, the Laurent series expansion of f(z) around z=0 has a term 24/z, so the residue is 24.\n\nTherefore, all methods point to the residue being 24. Therefore, the answer is 24. So the residue at z=0 is \\boxed{24}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the residue at \\( z = 0 \\) for the function \n\n$$\nf(z) = \\frac{\\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z}{(\\sin z - z) \\sin z},\n$$\n\nwe start by analyzing the Taylor series expansions of the numerator and denominator around \\( z = 0 \\).\n\n**Numerator Analysis:**\nThe Taylor series expansions for \\( \\sin 3z \\) and \\( \\sin z \\) are:\n$$\n\\sin 3z = 3z - \\frac{(3z)^3}{6} + \\frac{(3z)^5}{120} - \\cdots = 3z - \\frac{9z^3}{2} + \\frac{81z^5}{40} - \\cdots,\n$$\n$$\n3 \\sin z = 3 \\left( z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\frac{z^5}{120} - \\cdots \\right) = 3z - \\frac{z^3}{2} + \\frac{z^5}{40} - \\cdots.\n$$\nSubtracting these, we get:\n$$\n\\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z = \\left( 3z - \\frac{9z^3}{2} + \\frac{81z^5}{40} - \\cdots \\right) - \\left( 3z - \\frac{z^3}{2} + \\frac{z^5}{40} - \\cdots \\right) = -4z^3 + 2z^5 - \\cdots.\n$$\n\n**Denominator Analysis:**\nThe Taylor series expansion for \\( \\sin z - z \\) is:\n$$\n\\sin z - z = \\left( z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\frac{z^5}{120} - \\cdots \\right) - z = -\\frac{z^3}{6} + \\frac{z^5}{120} - \\cdots,\n$$\nand for \\( \\sin z \\):\n$$\n\\sin z = z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\frac{z^5}{120} - \\cdots.\n$$\nThus, the denominator is:\n$$\n(\\sin z - z) \\sin z = \\left( -\\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots \\right) \\left( z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots \\right) = -\\frac{z^4}{6} + \\cdots.\n$$\n\n**Residue Calculation:**\nThe function \\( f(z) \\) has a simple pole at \\( z = 0 \\). The residue at a simple pole \\( z = a \\) is given by:\n$$\n\\text{Res}(f, 0) = \\lim_{z \\to 0} z \\cdot f(z).\n$$\nSubstituting the expansions into \\( f(z) \\):\n$$\nz \\cdot f(z) = \\frac{z (\\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z)}{(\\sin z - z) \\sin z} \\approx \\frac{z (-4z^3 + 2z^5)}{(-\\frac{z^4}{6})} = \\frac{-4z^4 + 2z^6}{-\\frac{z^4}{6}} = \\frac{-4}{-\\frac{1}{6}} = 24.\n$$\n\nThus, the residue at \\( z = 0 \\) is \n\n$$\n\\boxed{24}.\n$$", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the residue at \\(z = 0\\) for the function \n\n\\[ f(z) = \\frac{\\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z}{(\\sin z - z) \\sin z}, \\]\n\nwe need to determine the orders of the zeros of the numerator and the denominator functions at \\(z = 0\\).\n\n1. **Taylor Series Expansion**: \n   Let's use the Taylor series expansion of \\(\\sin z\\) around \\(z = 0\\):\n\n   \\[ \\sin z = z - \\frac{z^3}{3!} + \\frac{z^5}{5!} - \\cdots \\]\n\n2. **Numerator Analysis**: \n   Consider the numerator \\(\\varphi(z) = \\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z\\):\n\n   Using the series expansion:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\sin 3z &= 3z - \\frac{(3z)^3}{3!} + \\frac{(3z)^5}{5!} - \\cdots \\\\\n   &= 3z - \\frac{27z^3}{6} + \\frac{243z^5}{120} - \\cdots\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore,\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\varphi(z) &= \\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z \\\\\n   &= \\left(3z - \\frac{27z^3}{6} + \\frac{243z^5}{120} - \\cdots \\right) - 3\\left(z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\frac{z^5}{120} - \\cdots\\right) \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{27z^3}{6} + 3\\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{24z^3}{6} + \\cdots \\\\\n   &= -4z^3 + \\cdots\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, we can write:\n\n   \\[ \\varphi(z) = -4z^3 + \\text{higher order terms} = z^3 \\varphi_1(z), \\]\n\n   where:\n\n   \\[ \\varphi_1(0) = -4 \\neq 0 \\]\n\n3. **Denominator Analysis**: \n   Consider the denominator \\(\\psi(z) = (\\sin z - z) \\sin z\\):\n\n   Using the series expansion:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\sin z - z &= \\left(z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots \\right) - z \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore,\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\psi(z) &= (\\sin z - z) \\sin z \\\\\n   &= \\left(-\\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots \\right) \\left(z - \\frac{z^3}{6} + \\cdots\\right) \\\\\n   &= -\\frac{z^3}{6} z + \\text{higher order terms} \\\\\n   &= - \\frac{z^4}{6} + \\text{higher order terms}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, we can write:\n\n   \\[ \\psi(z) = -\\frac{z^4}{6} + \\text{higher order terms} = z^4 \\psi_1(z), \\]\n\n   where:\n\n   \\[ \\psi_1(0) = -\\frac{1}{6} \\neq 0 \\]\n\n4. **Function Simplification**: \n   Now, substituting these expressions into \\(f(z)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   f(z) = \\frac{\\sin 3z - 3 \\sin z}{(\\sin z - z) \\sin z} &= \\frac{z^3 \\varphi_1(z)}{z^4 \\psi_1(z)} \\\\\n   &= \\frac{\\varphi_1(z)}{z \\psi_1(z)}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Residue Computation**: \n   Because \\(\\varphi_1(0) \\neq 0\\) and \\(\\psi_1(0) \\neq 0\\), \\(z = 0\\) is a simple pole. The residue can be found using the limit formula:\n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{res}_{z=0} f(z) = \\lim_{z \\to 0} \\frac{\\varphi_1(z)}{z \\psi_1(z)} z = \\frac{\\varphi_1(0)}{\\psi_1(0)}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the values:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\operatorname{res}_{z=0} f(z) &= \\frac{\\varphi_1(0)}{\\psi_1(0)} \\\\\n   &= \\frac{-4}{-1/6} \\\\\n   &= 24\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the residue at \\(z = 0\\) is \\( \\boxed{24} \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "To store a password written in a 32-letter alphabet (where \"\u0438\" is identified with \"\u0439\"), each letter is represented by its ordinal number -- a pair of digits (e.g., A - 01, \u0411 - 02, etc.). This results in a sequence of digits \\( y_1, y_2, \\ldots \\). At the same time, according to the rule \\( x_{i+1} = r_{10}(a x_i + b), i \\in \\mathbb{N} \\), a sequence of decimal digits \\( (x_i) \\) is generated, where \\( r_{10}(x) \\) is the remainder of dividing \\( x \\) by 10, and \\( a, b \\) are natural numbers. The minimal period of this seq