ms complicated, but perhaps we can make progress by assuming possible values for a and b. Since the possible a's that give period 10 are likely a=1,11,21,... and b must be coprime with 10 (1,3,7,9). Let's first try a=1 and b=1.\n\n   If a=1 and b=1, then the LCG is x_{i+1} = (x_i +1) mod10. This would generate a sequence like x\u2081, x\u2081+1, x\u2081+2, ..., mod10. The period is 10 as long as the increment b=1 is coprime with 10 (which it is). Let's check if this works.\n\n   Let's assume a=1 and b=1. Then:\n\n   x\u2082 = x\u2081 +1 mod10\n\n   x\u2083 = x\u2082 +1 = x\u2081 +2 mod10\n\n   x\u2084 = x\u2081 +3 mod10\n\n   ...\n\n   x_i = x\u2081 + (i-1) mod10\n\n   Therefore, x_i = (x\u2081 + i -1) mod10 for i=1 to 10.\n\n   Then, since the period is 10, x\u2081\u2081 = x\u2081, which would be (x\u2081 +10) mod10 = x\u2081, which holds.\n\n   Now, let's substitute this into the equations.\n\n   For example, x\u2081 is a variable. Let's denote x\u2081 as s (seed). Then:\n\n   x\u2081 = s\n\n   x\u2082 = s+1 mod10\n\n   x\u2083 = s+2 mod10\n\n   x\u2084 = s+3 mod10\n\n   x\u2085 = s+4 mod10\n\n   x\u2086 = s+5 mod10\n\n   x\u2087 = s+6 mod10\n\n   x\u2088 = s+7 mod10\n\n   x\u2089 = s+8 mod10\n\n   x\u2081\u2080 = s+9 mod10\n\n   x\u2081\u2081 = s+10 mod10 = s\n\n   Now, substituting these into the equations:\n\n   First 8 equations:\n\n   1. s + y\u2081 \u22612 mod10 \u2192 y\u2081 = (2 - s) mod10\n\n   2. (s+1) + y\u2082 \u22618 mod10 \u2192 y\u2082 = (8 - (s+1)) mod10 = (7 - s) mod10\n\n   3. (s+2) + y\u2083 \u22615 mod10 \u2192 y\u2083 = (5 - (s+2)) mod10 = (3 - s) mod10\n\n   4. (s+3) + y\u2084 \u22612 mod10 \u2192 y\u2084 = (2 - (s+3)) mod10 = (-1 -s) mod10 = (9 -s) mod10\n\n   5. (s+4) + y\u2085 \u22618 mod10 \u2192 y\u2085 = (8 - (s+4)) mod10 = (4 - s) mod10\n\n   6. (s+5) + y\u2086 \u22613 mod10 \u2192 y\u2086 = (3 - (s+5)) mod10 = (-2 -s) mod10 = (8 -s) mod10\n\n   7. (s+6) + y\u2087 \u22611 mod10 \u2192 y\u2087 = (1 - (s+6)) mod10 = (-5 -s) mod10 = (5 -s) mod10\n\n   8. (s+7) + y\u2088 \u22619 mod10 \u2192 y\u2088 = (9 - (s+7)) mod10 = (2 -s) mod10\n\n   Now, the next 8 equations (9-16):\n\n   9. x\u2089 + y\u2081 \u22618 mod10 \u2192 (s+8) + y\u2081 \u22618 mod10 \u2192 y\u2081 \u2261 (8 - (s+8)) mod10 \u2192 y\u2081 \u2261 (-s) mod10 \u2192 y\u2081 \u2261 (10 -s) mod10\n\n   But from equation1, y\u2081 \u2261 (2 -s) mod10. Therefore:\n\n   10 -s \u22612 -s mod10 \u2192 10 \u22612 mod10 \u2192 8\u22610 mod10 \u2192 Which is not possible. Contradiction. So this assumption (a=1, b=1) leads to a contradiction. Therefore, a=1, b=1 is invalid.\n\n   Let's try another b value. Let's try a=1, b=3 (coprime to 10). Then the LCG is x_{i+1} = (x_i +3) mod10.\n\n   Then, x\u2082 = (x\u2081 +3) mod10\n\n   x\u2083 = (x\u2082 +3) = x\u2081 +6 mod10\n\n   x\u2084 = x\u2081 +9 mod10\n\n   x\u2085 = x\u2081 +12 mod10 \u2192 x\u2081 +2 mod10\n\n   x\u2086 = x\u2081 +5 mod10\n\n   x\u2087 = x\u2081 +8 mod10\n\n   x\u2088 = x\u2081 +11 mod10 \u2192 x\u2081 +1 mod10\n\n   x\u2089 = x\u2081 +4 mod10\n\n   x\u2081\u2080 = x\u2081 +7 mod10\n\n   Then, x\u2081\u2081 = (x\u2081\u2080 +3) mod10 = (x\u2081 +7 +3) mod10 = x\u2081 +10 mod10 = x\u2081 mod10 \u2192 x\u2081\u2081 =x\u2081, which is correct for period 10.\n\n   Now, substituting into equations:\n\n   First 8 equations:\n\n   x\u2081 = s\n\n   x\u2082 = s+3\n\n   x\u2083 = s+6\n\n   x\u2084 = s+9 mod10 \u2192 s+9-10k, but since s is between 0-9, x\u2084 = s+9 if s+9<10, else s+9-10. But perhaps better to keep as (s+9) mod10.\n\n   But let's keep them as expressions modulo10.\n\n   1. x\u2081 + y\u2081 \u22612 \u2192 s + y\u2081 \u22612 \u2192 y\u2081=(2 -s) mod10\n\n   2. x\u2082 + y\u2082 \u22618 \u2192 (s+3) + y\u2082 \u22618 \u2192 y\u2082=(8 -s -3)=5 -s mod10\n\n   3. x\u2083 + y\u2083 \u22615 \u2192 (s+6) + y\u2083 \u22615 \u2192 y\u2083=(5 -s -6)=(-s -1) mod10=9 -s mod10\n\n   4. x\u2084 + y\u2084 \u22612 \u2192 (s+9) + y\u2084 \u22612 \u2192 y\u2084=(2 -s -9)= (-s -7) mod10