oid), h\u00b2 must be positive, so k = h\u00b2 -16 \u2265 -16. So possible roots are k=-16 and k=112. But k=-16 would give h\u00b2=0, which would make the trapezoid degenerate (height zero). Therefore, the only meaningful root is k=112. Therefore, h\u00b2 -16=112 \u21d2 h\u00b2=128 \u21d2 h=\u221a128=8\u221a2.\n\nTherefore, h=8\u221a2. Then, b=(h\u00b2 -16)/(2h)= (128 -16)/(2*8\u221a2)= (112)/(16\u221a2)=7/\u221a2= (7\u221a2)/2.\n\nSo, the center of the circle \u0393 is at (4, (7\u221a2)/2), radius r= sqrt(16 + b\u00b2). Let's compute b\u00b2:\n\nb=7\u221a2/2 \u21d2 b\u00b2= (49*2)/4= 98/4=49/2. Thus, r\u00b2=16 +49/2= (32 +49)/2=81/2 \u21d2 r=9/\u221a2= (9\u221a2)/2.\n\nNow, confirming the distance from center to line CD is equal to radius.\n\nThe equation of line CD is y=(h/14)(x -8). With h=8\u221a2, this is y=(8\u221a2/14)(x -8)= (4\u221a2/7)(x -8).\n\nExpressed as: 4\u221a2 x -7 y -32\u221a2 =0.\n\nThe distance from center (4, (7\u221a2)/2) to this line is |4\u221a2*4 -7*(7\u221a2)/2 -32\u221a2| / sqrt( (4\u221a2)^2 + (-7)^2 ).\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n16\u221a2 - (49\u221a2)/2 -32\u221a2.\n\nConvert all terms to halves:\n\n(32\u221a2 -49\u221a2 -64\u221a2)/2 = (-81\u221a2)/2.\n\nAbsolute value is (81\u221a2)/2.\n\nDenominator:\n\nsqrt( (16*2) +49 )= sqrt(32 +49)=sqrt(81)=9.\n\nTherefore, distance is (81\u221a2)/2 /9= (9\u221a2)/2, which is equal to the radius r=9\u221a2/2. So this checks out.\n\nTherefore, h=8\u221a2. Therefore, the legs AB and CD have lengths:\n\nAB= sqrt( (4 -0)^2 + (8\u221a2 -0)^2 )= sqrt(16 + 128)=sqrt(144)=12.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, point B is at (4, h)= (4, 8\u221a2). So, AB is from (0,0) to (4,8\u221a2). So, AB length is sqrt( (4)^2 + (8\u221a2)^2 )= sqrt(16 + 64*2)= sqrt(16 +128)=sqrt(144)=12. Correct.\n\nCD is from (8,0) to (22,8\u221a2). So, the horizontal distance is 22 -8=14, vertical distance is 8\u221a2. Therefore, CD length is sqrt(14^2 + (8\u221a2)^2)= sqrt(196 + 128)=sqrt(324)=18.\n\nSo, perimeter is AB + BC + CD + DA= 12 +18