Therefore, p=49, q=-25 or p=-25, q=49.\n\nSo then, the factors would be:\n\n(12w^2 +49w +12)(12w^2 -25w +12)\n\nLet me check if this multiplication gives the original quartic.\n\nFirst, multiply (12w^2 +49w +12)(12w^2 -25w +12):\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\n12w^2*(12w^2) =144w^4\n\n12w^2*(-25w)= -300w^3\n\n12w^2*12=144w^2\n\n49w*(12w^2)=588w^3\n\n49w*(-25w)= -1225w^2\n\n49w*12=588w\n\n12*(12w^2)=144w^2\n\n12*(-25w)= -300w\n\n12*12=144\n\nNow, sum all terms:\n\n144w^4 + (-300w^3 +588w^3) + (144w^2 -1225w^2 +144w^2) + (588w -300w) +144\n\nSimplify:\n\n144w^4 +288w^3 +(-937w^2) +288w +144\n\nWhich matches the original quartic equation. Perfect! So the quartic factors as:\n\n(12w^2 +49w +12)(12w^2 -25w +12)=0\n\nTherefore, set each quadratic equal to zero:\n\nFirst quadratic: 12w^2 +49w +12=0\n\nSolutions:\n\nw = [-49 \u00b1sqrt(49^2 -4*12*12)]/(2*12)\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n49^2 -4*12*12 =2401 -576=1825\n\nsqrt(1825). Let's compute sqrt(1825). 1825=25*73, so sqrt(1825)=5*sqrt(73). Therefore,\n\nw = [-49 \u00b15sqrt(73)]/24\n\nBut since w was defined as sqrt(u^2 -1), and u >1, then w must be positive. So we check if these solutions can be positive:\n\n[-49 +5sqrt(73)]/24 \u2248 [-49 +5*8.544]/24 \u2248 [-49 +42.72]/24 \u2248 (-6.28)/24 \u2248 negative\n\n[-49 -5sqrt(73)]/24 is also negative. So both solutions are negative. Since w must be positive, these are extraneous. Therefore, no solutions from the first quadratic.\n\nSecond quadratic:12w^2 -25w +12=0\n\nSolutions:\n\nw = [25 \u00b1sqrt(625 -4*12*12)]/(2*12)\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n625 -576=49\n\nsqrt(49)=7\n\nThus,\n\nw=(25 \u00b17)/24\n\nSo two solutions:\n\nw=(25+7)/24=32/24=4/3\u22481.333\n\nw=(25-7)/24=18/24=3/4=0.75\n\nBoth positive. So possible solutions. So w=4/3 or w=3/4.\n\nNow, recall that w = sqrt(u^2 -1). So:\n\nCase 1: w=4/3\n\nThen, sqrt(u^2 -1)=4/3\n\nSquare both sides: u^2 -1=16/9\n\nTherefore, u^2=16/9 +1=25/9\n\nThus, u=5/3 or u=-5/3. But since u =x/3 and x>3, u>1. So u=5/3 is acceptable, 