ll}, {"problem": "A certain mechanism consists of 25 parts, some of which are large and some small. It is known that among any 12 selected parts, there will necessarily be at least one small part, and among any 15 parts, there will necessarily be at least one large part. How many of each type of parts does the mechanism contain?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this problem step by step. So, the problem is about a mechanism that has 25 parts, some large and some small. The conditions are:\n\n1. Among any 12 selected parts, there must be at least one small part.\n2. Among any 15 selected parts, there must be at least one large part.\n\nWe need to find out how many large and small parts there are. Hmm, let's start by breaking down the problem.\n\nFirst, let's denote the number of large parts as L and the number of small parts as S. Since the total number of parts is 25, we have the equation:\n\nL + S = 25\n\nNow, the first condition says that in any group of 12 parts, there's at least one small part. That means that it's impossible to have a group of 12 parts that are all large. So, the maximum number of large parts must be less than 12. Wait, but if we have 12 large parts, then selecting those 12 would violate the first condition. Therefore, the maximum number of large parts L must be 11. Because if L is 11, then even if we tried to pick 12 parts, we couldn't have all large ones since there are only 11. So, that would satisfy the first condition. So, does that mean L \u2264 11?\n\nWait, but let me think again. If there are L large parts, then the maximum number of large parts we can have is 11 because 12 would mean that there exists a group of 12 with all large parts. Therefore, yes, L must be \u2264 11.\n\nSimilarly, the second condition states that among any 15 parts, there is at least one large part. That implies that you can't have 15 small parts. So, the maximum number of small parts S must be less than 15. Therefore, S \u2264 14. Since S = 25 - L, then substituting, we get 25 - L \u2264 14, which means L \u2265 11.\n\nWait, that's interesting. From the first condition, L \u2264 11. From the second condition, L \u2265 11. Therefore, combining these two, L must be exactly 11. Therefore, S = 25 - 11 = 14.\n\nBut let me verify this because sometimes these combinatorial problems can be tricky. Let me check both conditions with L = 11 and S = 14.\n\nFirst condition: Any 12 parts must include at least one small. Since there are 11 large parts, the maximum number of large parts you can have in any selection is 11, so if you pick 12 parts, you have to include at least one small part. That works.\n\nSecond condition: Any 15 parts must include at least one large. Since there are 14 small parts, the maximum number of small parts you can have is 14, so if you pick 15 parts, you have to include at least one large part. That also works.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 11 large parts and 14 small parts. But let me just make sure there's no other possibility. Suppose L = 10, then S = 15. But wait, S = 15 would violate the second condition because if you can have 15 small parts, then selecting those 15 would have no large parts. So S can't be 15, hence L can't be 10. Similarly, if L is 12, but that's already prohibited by the first condition. So yeah, the only possible solution is L = 11 and S = 14.\n\nI think that's solid. Let me just recap:\n\n- To ensure any 12 parts have at least one small, there can't be 12 or more large parts. So maximum L is 11.\n\n- To ensure any 15 parts have at least one large, there can't be 15 or more small parts. So maximum S is 14, which translates to minimum L of 11 (since 25 - 14 = 11).\n\nTherefore, L must be exactly 11 and S exactly 14. That satisfies both conditions. I don't see any loopholes here. The constraints are complementary, pushing L to be exactly 11. So, the answer is 11 large and 14 small parts.", "deepseek_solution": "The mechanism contains **11 large parts** and **1