tween each pair of consecutive roots, the sign alternates. So the sign alternates each time we cross a root. Since the first interval (leftmost) is positive, then the next interval (between first and second root) is negative, then positive, then negative, etc. So, the intervals where P(x) is negative are the intervals between the 1st and 2nd roots, 3rd and 4th roots, 5th and 6th, etc. So between (1\u00b2, 2\u00b2), (3\u00b2, 4\u00b2), (5\u00b2, 6\u00b2), ..., (99\u00b2, 100\u00b2). Because each time, crossing a root flips the sign. Since there are 100 roots, there are 99 intervals between them. The sign alternates between each interval. Since the first interval (left of first root) is positive, then the first interval between 1 and 4 is negative, the next between 4 and 9 is positive, etc. So the negative intervals are every other interval starting from the first one. So, the intervals (k\u00b2, (k+1)\u00b2) where k is odd. But since 1 is odd, then 1-2, 3-4, etc. So, k = 1,3,5,...,99. Therefore, how many negative intervals are there? From k=1 to k=99, stepping by 2. That's 50 intervals. Wait, 1 to 99 inclusive, step 2: (99 -1)/2 +1 = 49 +1 = 50. So there are 50 intervals where the polynomial is negative.\n\nBut the question is about integers n where P(n) \u2264 0. So that includes both the negative intervals and the roots themselves. The roots are the squares, so each square is an integer. Therefore, the integers where P(n) =0 are exactly the squares from 1\u00b2 to 100\u00b2, which are 100 integers. Then, the integers where P(n) <0 are the integers lying in the open intervals (k\u00b2, (k+1)^2) where the polynomial is negative. But which intervals are negative? As we determined, between 1\u00b2 and 2\u00b2 (i.e., 1 to 4), 3\u00b2 to 4\u00b2 (9 to 16), etc., so every interval where k is odd. \n\nWait, but let me clarify. The intervals where P(x) is negative are (1,4), (9,16), (25,36), ..., (99\u00b2, 100\u00b2). So, each interval between an odd square and the next ev