\nTherefore, the sum of the squares is:\n\n(y\u2081 + y\u2082 + y\u2083)\u00b2 - 2(y\u2081y\u2082 + y\u2081y\u2083 + y\u2082y\u2083) = (-p/q)\u00b2 - 2*0 = (p\u00b2)/(q\u00b2)\n\nWhich is exactly the same result as before. Therefore, this confirms that the sum 1/z\u2081\u00b2 + 1/z\u2082\u00b2 + 1/z\u2083\u00b2 equals (p/q)\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be p\u00b2/q\u00b2, written as \\boxed{\\dfrac{p^2}{q^2}}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe value of $\\frac{1}{z_{1}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{z_{2}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{z_{3}^{2}}$ is \\boxed{\\dfrac{p^2}{q^2}}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given the polynomial \\(X^3 + pX + q\\) with roots \\(z_1, z_2, z_3\\), we need to calculate \\(\\frac{1}{z_1^2} + \\frac{1}{z_2^2} + \\frac{1}{z_3^2}\\) in terms of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\).\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas for the polynomial \\(X^3 + pX + q\\), we have:\n\\[\nz_1 + z_2 + z_3 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\nz_1z_2 + z_1z_3 + z_2z_3 = p,\n\\]\n\\[\nz_1z_2z_3 = -q.\n\\]\n\nWe start by finding the sum of the reciprocals of the roots:\n\\[\nS = \\frac{1}{z_1} + \\frac{1}{z_2} + \\frac{1}{z_3} = \\frac{z_2z_3 + z_1z_3 + z_1z_2}{z_1z_2z_3} = \\frac{p}{-q} = -\\frac{p}{q}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we consider the sum of the reciprocals of the products of the roots taken two at a time:\n\\[\nT = \\frac{1}{z_1z_2} + \\frac{1}{z_1z_3} + \\frac{1}{z_2z_3} = \\frac{z_1 + z_2 + z_3}{z_1z_2z_3} = \\frac{0}{-q} = 0.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the squares of the reciprocals of the roots can be calculated using the identity:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{z_1} + \\frac{1}{z_2} + \\frac{1}{z_3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{z_1^2} + \\frac{1}{z_2^2} + \\frac{1}{z_3^2} + 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{z_1z_2} + \\frac{1}{z_1z_3} + \\frac{1}{z_2z_3} \\right).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(S\\) and \\(T\\) into this identity, we get:\n\\[\n\\left( -\\frac{p}{q} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{z_1^2} + \\frac{1}{z_2^2} + \\frac{1}{z_3^2} + 2 \\cdot 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{p^2}{q^2} = \\frac{1}{z_1^2} + \\frac{1}{z_2^2} + \\frac{1}{z_3^2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\frac{1}