uadratic polynomial would not overlap. So, actually, maybe I can still try a quadratic particular solution. Let me check.\n\nAssume a particular solution a_n^p = An\u00b2 + Bn + C.\n\nThen, compute a_{n+2}^p - 2a_{n+1}^p + a_n^p.\n\nCompute each term:\n\na_{n+2}^p = A(n+2)\u00b2 + B(n+2) + C = A(n\u00b2 + 4n + 4) + Bn + 2B + C = An\u00b2 + (4A + B)n + (4A + 2B + C)\n\na_{n+1}^p = A(n+1)\u00b2 + B(n+1) + C = A(n\u00b2 + 2n + 1) + Bn + B + C = An\u00b2 + (2A + B)n + (A + B + C)\n\nSo, 2a_{n+1}^p = 2An\u00b2 + 2(2A + B)n + 2(A + B + C)\n\nThen, a_{n+2}^p - 2a_{n+1}^p + a_n^p = [An\u00b2 + (4A + B)n + (4A + 2B + C)] - [2An\u00b2 + 2(2A + B)n + 2(A + B + C)] + [An\u00b2 + Bn + C]\n\nLet's compute term by term:\n\nFirst, the n\u00b2 terms: An\u00b2 - 2An\u00b2 + An\u00b2 = 0.\n\nThe n terms: (4A + B)n - 2(2A + B)n + Bn = [4A + B - 4A - 2B + B]n = 0.\n\nConstant terms: (4A + 2B + C) - 2(A + B + C) + C = 4A + 2B + C - 2A - 2B - 2C + C = (4A - 2A) + (2B - 2B) + (C - 2C + C) = 2A + 0 + 0 = 2A.\n\nSo, the entire left-hand side of the recurrence becomes 2A. But according to the equation, this should equal n - 20. So, 2A = n - 20.\n\nWait, that's a problem. The left-hand side is a constant 2A, but the right-hand side is linear in n. This suggests that our initial assumption for the particular solution was incorrect. Therefore, a quadratic particular solution isn't sufficient. Hmm, maybe we need to try a cubic polynomial?\n\nWait, no, let's think again. When the nonhomogeneous term is a polynomial of degree d, and the homogeneous solution includes terms up to a certain degree, we multiply by n^k where k is the multiplicity of the root. Since the homogeneous solution here is a linear polynomial (degree 1), and the nonhomogeneous term is a linear polynomial (degree 1), we need to multiply by n\u00b2. So, let's try a particular solution of the form a_n^p = An\u00b3 + Bn\u00b2 + Cn + D.\n\nLet me attempt this.\n\nAssume a particular solution a_n^p = An\u00b3 + Bn\u00b2 + Cn + D.\n\nC