:540/20=27\n\n22:540/22\u224824.54\u2192no\n\nEtc.\n\nCompute the expression:\n\nAB + AC + BC +2(A+B+C)=14B +14C + BC +2(14 + B + C)=14B +14C + BC +28 +2B +2C= BC +16B +16C +28=1496\n\nThus:\n\nBC +16B +16C +28=1496 \u21d2 BC +16B +16C=1468\n\nBut BC=540, so:\n\n540 +16B +16C=1468 \u21d216B +16C=928 \u21d2B + C=928/16=58.\n\nSo, B + C=58, B*C=540. Solve x\u00b2 -58x +540=0.\n\nDiscriminant D=3364 -2160=1204, which is not a perfect square. So no integer solutions. Hence, A=14 invalid.\n\nContinuing this way would take a long time, but since we already found a valid solution with A=10, B=18, C=42 leading to primes 11,19,43 which satisfy all conditions, and given the complexity of the problem, this is likely the only solution. Therefore, the number is 8987.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number is \\boxed{8987}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given a number \\( N \\) which is the product of three prime factors \\( p, q, r \\), we need to find \\( N \\) such that:\n\n1. The sum of the squares of its three prime factors is 2331.\n2. The Euler's totient function \\( \\varphi(N) = 7560 \\).\n3. The sum of all divisors \\( \\sigma(N) = 10560 \\).\n\nWe denote the primes as \\( p, q, r \\). The conditions translate to:\n1. \\( p^2 + q^2 + r^2 = 2331 \\)\n2. \\( (p-1)(q-1)(r-1) = 7560 \\)\n3. \\( (1 + p)(1 + q)(1 + r) = 10560 \\)\n\nWe start by considering the factorization of 7560 and 10560. After systematic analysis and solving the equations, we find:\n\n1. Let \\( A = p - 1 \\), \\( B = q - 1 \\), \\( C = r - 1 \\). Then \\( A \\cdot B \\cdot C = 7560 \\).\n2. Expanding the sum of divisors condition, we find \\( (A + 2)(B + 2)(C + 2) = 10560 \\).\n\nAfter solving these equations, we find the primes \\( p, q, r \\) as follows:\n- \\( A = 10 \\), \\( B = 18 \\), \\( C = 42 \\)\n- Converting back to primes: \\( p = A + 1 = 11 \\), \\( q = B + 1 = 19 \\), \\( r = C + 1 = 43 \\)\n\nVerification:\n1. Sum of squares: \\( 11^2 + 19^2 + 43^2 = 121 + 361 + 1849 = 2331 \\)\n2. Euler's totient function: \\( (11-1)(19-1)(43-1) = 10 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 42 = 7560 \\)\n3. Sum of divisors: \\( (1 + 11)(1 + 19)(1 + 43) = 12 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 44 = 10560 \\)\n\nThus, the number \\( N = 11 \\times 19 \\times 43 = 8987 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8987}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let $N = p \\cdot q \\cdot r$, where $p$, $q$, and $r$ are prime numbers.\n\n2. According to the problem, we know that:\n   \\[\n   p^2 + q^2 + r^2 = 2331\n   \\]\n   Since $p$, $q$, and $r$ are primes and $p^2 + q^2 + r^2 = 2331$, each prime must be less than the square root of 2331:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{2331} \\approx 48.28\n   \\]\n   Thus, $p, q, r < 49$. Also, since all prime numbers greater than 2 are odd, $p, q,$ and $r$ must all be odd.\n\n3. The sum of all the divisors of $N$ is given as:\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(N) = (1 + p)(1 + q)(1 + r) = 10560\n   \\]\n   We can rewrite $10560$ as:\n   \\[\n   10560 = 11 \\times 960\n   \\]\n   To get the prime numbers, note that the only multiple of 11 not exceeding 49 and greater by 1 than a prime number is:\n   \\[\n   44 = 1 + 43 \\implies r = 43\n   \\]\n\n4. Substituting $r = 43$, we now have:\n   \\[\n   p^2 + q^2 + 43^2 = 2331\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   p^2 + q^2 + 1849 = 2331\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   p^2 + q^2 = 482\n   \\]\n\n5. Both $p$ and $q$ are primes and less than $\\sqrt{482}$:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{482} \\approx 21.97 \\implies p, q < 22\n   \\]\n   Notice that the square of an odd prime number ends in either 1 or 9. Thus, checking each possibility for the primes under 22, we test which pairs $(p, q)$ satisfy $p^2 + q^2 = 482$.\n\n6. By trials or factorization, we find that:\n   \\[\n   11^2 + 19^2 = 121 + 361 = 482\n   \\]\n   Thus, $p = 11$ and $q = 19$.\n\n7. Finally, we calculate \\(N\\):\n   \\[\n   N = 11 \\times 19 \\times 43\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N = 11 \\times 19 = 209\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N = 209 \\times 43 = 8987\n   \\]\n\nConclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{8987}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": nu