f so, the center of homothety would map EFGH to E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081. But ABCD is sandwiched between them. \n\nAlternatively, since ABCD is between EFGH and E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081, and the sides of E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081 are parallel to EFGH, perhaps ABCD is a parallelogram? Not necessarily. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe we can model this with coordinates. Let me assign coordinates to E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081. Let's suppose E\u2081 is at (0,0), F\u2081 at (a,0), G\u2081 at (b,c), H\u2081 at (d,e). Then, since E\u2081F\u2081 is parallel to EF, which would be a translation or scaling. But maybe this will get too complicated. Alternatively, set up coordinates such that EFGH is a parallelogram? Wait, but the problem states all quadrilaterals are convex, not necessarily parallelograms.\n\nAlternatively, let's use barycentric coordinates or affine transformations. But perhaps it's easier to assign coordinates in such a way that the ratios can be easily calculated.\n\nLet me consider E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081. Let's set E\u2081 at (0,0), F\u2081 at (1,0), G\u2081 at (1,1), H\u2081 at (0,1), making E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081 a square. Then the sides are parallel to the axes, so EF, FG, GH, HE would need to be parallel to the sides of the square. But the problem says E\u2081F\u2081 || EF, etc. So if E\u2081F\u2081 is along the x-axis, then EF is also along the x-axis, and so on. So EFGH would also be a square? But they could be rectangles with the same orientation.\n\nWait, but the problem states that E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081 is a convex quadrilateral with sides parallel to EFGH. So if EFGH is arbitrary, E\u2081F\u2081G\u2081H\u2081 is another convex quadrilateral with corresponding sides parallel. So maybe they are similar? Not necessarily, since similarity requires equal angles and proportional sides, but here only parallelism is given. So they are related by an affine transformation. Since affine transformations preserve ratios along lines and paral