ngle ADC}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{F_1C}{CG_1} = \\frac{E_1A}{AH_1} = \\lambda\n   \\]\n3. **Conclusion**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\lambda}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Solve the following equation in the set of real numbers:\n\n\\[ 8^{x}+27^{x}+2 \\cdot 30^{x}+54^{x}+60^{x}=12^{x}+18^{x}+20^{x}+24^{x}+45^{x}+90^{x} \\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this equation to solve in real numbers: \n\n\\[ 8^{x} + 27^{x} + 2 \\cdot 30^{x} + 54^{x} + 60^{x} = 12^{x} + 18^{x} + 20^{x} + 24^{x} + 45^{x} + 90^{x} \\]\n\nHmm, that's a bit intimidating with all those exponents. Let me try to break it down step by step. First, I notice that all the bases are composite numbers. Maybe I can factor them into primes to see if there's a pattern or common factors. Let's list each term with its prime factors:\n\nLeft Side:\n- 8 = 2\u00b3\n- 27 = 3\u00b3\n- 30 = 2\u00d73\u00d75 (and it's multiplied by 2, so 2\u00d730^x = 2^(x+1)\u00d73^x\u00d75^x)\nWait, maybe factoring each term into primes will help? Let me try that.\n\nLeft Side Terms:\n1. 8^x = (2\u00b3)^x = 2^{3x}\n2. 27^x = (3\u00b3)^x = 3^{3x}\n3. 2\u00b730^x = 2\u00b7(2\u00d73\u00d75)^x = 2^{x+1}\u00b73^x\u00b75^x\n4. 54^x = (2\u00d73\u00b3)^x = 2^x\u00b73^{3x}\n5. 60^x = (2\u00b2\u00d73\u00d75)^x = 2^{2x}\u00b73^x\u00b75^x\n\nRight Side Terms:\n1. 12^x = (2\u00b2\u00d73)^x = 2^{2x}\u00b73^x\n2. 18^x = (2\u00d73\u00b2)^x = 2^x\u00b73^{2x}\n3. 20^x = (2\u00b2\u00d75)^x = 2^{2x}\u00b75^x\n4. 24^x = (2\u00b3\u00d73)^x = 2^{3x}\u00b73^x\n5. 45^x = (3\u00b2\u00d75)^x = 3^{2x}\u00b75^x\n6. 90^x = (2\u00d73\u00b2\u00d75)^x = 2^x\u00b73^{2x}\u00b75^x\n\nHmm, so after factoring, both sides have terms with exponents involving 2, 3, 5 raised to various powers multiplied together. Maybe if I can express all terms in terms of some common variables, like a = 2^x, b = 3^x, c = 5^x. Let me try substituting that.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\na = 2^x,\nb = 3^x,\nc = 5^x.\n\nThen, rewriting all terms:\n\nLeft Side:\n1. 8^x = (2\u00b3)^x = a\u00b3\n2. 27^x = (3\u00b3)^x = b\u00b3\n3. 2\u00b730^x = 2\u00b7(2\u00d73\u00d75)^x = 2\u00b7a\u00b7b\u00b7c = 2abc\n4. 54^x = (2\u00d73\u00b3)^x = a\u00b7b\u00b3\n5. 60^x = (2\u00b2\u00d73\u00d75)^x = a\u00b2\u00b7b\u00b7c\n\nRight Side:\n1. 12^x = (2\u00b2\u00d73)^x = a\u00b2\u00b7b\n2. 18^x = (2\u00d73\u00b2)^x = a\u00b7b\u00b2\n3. 20^x = (2\u00b2\u00d75)^x = a\u00b2\u00b7c\n4. 24^x = (2\u00b3\u00d73)^x = a\u00b3\u00b7b\n5. 45^x = (3\u00b2\u00d75)^x = b\u00b2\u00b7c\n6. 90^x = (2\u00d73\u00b2\u00d75)^x = a\u00b7b\u00b2\u00b7c\n\nSo substituting all that, the equation becomes:\n\nLeft Side: a\u00b3 + b\u00b3 + 2abc + a\u00b7b\u00b3 + a\u00b2\u00b7b\u00b7c\n\nRight Side: a\u00b2\u00b7b + a\u00b7b\u00b2 + a\u00b2\u00b7c + a\u00b3\u00b7b + b\u00b2\u00b7c + a\u00b7b\u00b2\u00b7c\n\nLet me write the entire equation with these substitutions:\n\na\u00b3 + b\u00b3 + 2abc + a b\u00b3 + a\u00b2 b c = a\u00b2 b + a b\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c + a\u00b3 b + b\u00b2 c + a b\u00b2 c\n\nHmm, this looks complicated. Maybe rearrange all terms to one side to see if there's a way to factor or find common terms.\n\nLeft Side - Right Side = 0:\n\na\u00b3 + b\u00b3 + 2abc + a b\u00b3 + a\u00b2 b c - a\u00b2 b - a b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c - a\u00b3 b - b\u00b2 c - a b\u00b2 c = 0\n\nLet me see if I can group similar terms.\n\nFirst, terms with a\u00b3:\n\na\u00b3 - a\u00b3 b = a\u00b3(1 - b)\n\nTerms with b\u00b3:\n\nb\u00b3 + a b\u00b3 = b\u00b3(1 + a)\n\nTerms with 2abc:\n\n2abc\n\nTerms with a\u00b2 b c:\n\na\u00b2 b c\n\nTerms with -a\u00b2 b:\n\n- a\u00b2 b\n\nTerms with -a b\u00b2:\n\n- a b\u00b2\n\nTerms with -a\u00b2 c:\n\n- a\u00b2 c\n\nTerms with -b\u00b2 c:\n\n- b\u00b2 c\n\nTerms with -a b\u00b2 c:\n\n- a b\u00b2 c\n\nSo combining all together:\n\na\u00b3(1 - b) + b\u00b3(1 + a) + 2abc + a\u00b2 b c - a\u00b2 b - a b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c - b\u00b2 c - a b\u00b2 c = 0\n\nThis still looks complicated. Maybe factor some terms.\n\nLet me look for common factors in groups.\n\nStarting with a\u00b3(1 - b) and -a\u00b2 b:\n\na\u00b3(1 - b) - a\u00b2 b = a\u00b2 [a(1 - b) - b] = a\u00b2 [a - a b - b]\n\nSimilarly, terms with b\u00b3(1 + a) and -a b\u00b2:\n\nb\u00b3(1 + a) - a b\u00b2 = b\u00b2 [b(1 + a) - a] = b\u00b2 [b + a b - a]\n\nTerms with 2abc + a\u00b2 b c - a\u00b2 c:\n\nTake 2abc + a\u00b2 b c - a\u00b2 c = a c (2b + a b - a)\n\nTerms with -b\u00b2 c - a b\u00b2 c:\n\n- b\u00b2 c (1 + a)\n\nHmm, this is getting more structured. Let's write all these parts:\n\n1. a\u00b2 [a - a b - b]\n2. b\u00b2 [b + a b - a]\n3. a c (2b + a b - a)\n4. - b\u00b2 c (1 + a)\n\nSo combining all four:\n\na\u00b2 [a - a b - b] + b\u00b2 [b + a b - a] + a c (2b + a b - a) - b\u00b2 c (1 + a) = 0\n\nThis still seems quite involved. Maybe there's a better approach. Let's consider specific values of x where the equation might hold. For example, x=0, x=1, or x=2. Let me test these.\n\nFirst, x=0:\n\nLeft Side: 8^0 +27^0 +2\u00b730^0 +54^0 +60^0 = 1 +1 +2\u00b71 +1 +1 = 1+1+2+1+1=6\n\nRight Side: 12^0 +18^0 +20^0 +24^0 +45^0 +90^0 =1+1+1+1+1+1=6\n\nSo, 6=6. Therefore, x=0 is a solution.\n\nNow check x=1:\n\nLeft Side:8 +27 +2\u00b730 +54 +60=8+27+60+54+60=8+27=35; 35+60=95; 95+54=149; 149+60=209\n\nRight Side:12 +18 +20 +24 +45 +90=12+18=30; 30+20=50;50+24=74;74+45=119;119+90=209\n\nSo, 209=209. Therefore, x=1 is also a solution.\n\nInteresting, both x=0 and x=1 satisfy the equation. Let's check x=2 to see if there's another solution.\n\nLeft Side:8\u00b2 +27\u00b2 +2\u00b730\u00b2 +54\u00b2 +60\u00b2=64 +729 +2\u00b7900 +2916 +3600\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n64 +729=793; 2\u00b7900=1800; 793+1800=2593; 2593+2916=5509; 5509+3600=9109\n\nRight Side:12\u00b2 +18\u00b2 +20\u00b2 +24\u00b2 +45\u00b2 +90\u00b2=144 +324 +400 +576 +2025 +8100\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n144 +324=468; 468+400=868; 868+576=1444; 1444+2025=3469; 3469+8100=11569\n\nSo Left Side=9109 vs Right Side=11569. Not equal. So x=2 is not a solution.\n\nWhat about x= -1?\n\nLeft Side:8^{-1} +27^{-1} +2\u00b730^{-1} +54^{-1} +60^{-1}=1/8 +1/27 +2/30 +1/54 +1/60\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n1/8 \u22480.125; 1/27\u22480.037; 2/30\u22480.0667; 1/54\u22480.0185; 1/60\u22480.0167\n\nAdding up: 0.125+0.037=0.162; +0.0667=0.2287; +0.0185=0.2472; +0.0167\u22480.2639\n\nRight Side:12^{-1} +18^{-1} +20^{-1} +24^{-1} +45^{-1} +90^{-1}=1/12 +1/18 +1/20 +1/24 +1/45 +1/90\n\nCalculate:\n\n1/12\u22480.0833; 1/18\u22480.0556; 1/20=0.05; 1/24\u22480.0417; 1/45\u22480.0222; 1/90\u22480.0111\n\nAdding up: 0.0833+0.0556\u22480.1389; +0.05=0.1889; +0.0417\u22480.2306; +0.0222\u22480.2528; +0.0111\u22480.2639\n\nSo both sides \u22480.2639. So x=-1 is also a solution.\n\nWait, really? Let me verify the calculations more accurately.\n\nLeft Side:\n\n1/8 = 0.125\n\n1/27 \u22480.037037\n\n2/30 = 1/15 \u22480.0666667\n\n1/54 \u22480.0185185\n\n1/60 \u22480.0166667\n\nAdding: 0.125 + 0.037037 \u22480.162037\n\n+0.0666667\u22480.2287037\n\n+0.0185185\u22480.2472222\n\n+0.0166667\u22480.2638889\n\nRight Side:\n\n1/12 \u22480.0833333\n\n1/18 \u22480.0555556\n\n1/20 =0.05\n\n1/24 \u22480.0416667\n\n1/45 \u22480.0222222\n\n1/90 \u22480.0111111\n\nAdding: 0.0833333 +0.0555556\u22480.1388889\n\n+0.05\u22480.1888889\n\n+0.0416667\u22480.2305556\n\n+0.0222222\u22480.2527778\n\n+0.0111111\u22480.2638889\n\nSo both sides are exactly equal to 0.263888... when x=-1. So x=-1 is a solution as well.\n\nSo far, we have three solutions: x=-1, x=0, x=1.\n\nWait, could there be more solutions? Let's check x=0.5 to see what happens.\n\nLeft Side:\n\n8^{0.5} +27^{0.5} +2\u00b730^{0.5} +54^{0.5} +60^{0.5}\n\nWhich is sqrt(8) + sqrt(27) + 2*sqrt(30) + sqrt(54) + sqrt(60)\n\nCalculate each:\n\nsqrt(8)=2\u221a2\u22482.828\n\nsqrt(27)=3\u221a3\u22485.196\n\nsqrt(30)\u22485.477; 2*5.477\u224810.954\n\nsqrt(54)=3\u221a6\u22487.348\n\nsqrt(60)=2\u221a15\u22487.746\n\nAdding up:\n\n2.828 +5.196\u22488.024\n\n+10.954\u224818.978\n\n+7.348\u224826.326\n\n+7.